VDI18N.add('en', 'notes/e-over-m', [
"e/m of the Electron: teaching notes",
"e/m of the Electron",
"Modern physics lab",
"{#} to {#} minutes (a full lab period with the table and fit)",
"A charge moving at right angles to a uniform field feels a force evB at right angles to its velocity, so it moves in a circle of radius r = mv/(eB). Speed comes from the gun: eV = ½mv².",
"Eliminating v gives e/m = 2V/(B²r²). Neither e nor m can be found separately this way, only their ratio, which is why Thomson measured e/m.",
"B at the center of a Helmholtz pair is ({#}/{#})^({#}/{#}) μ₀NI/a, nearly uniform over the middle; the coil radius a is given in the manual ({#} mm for the PASCO SE-{#}) and can be checked with a ruler.",
"Reading r is the hard part: parallax between the beam and the scale, the beam's width, and choosing the edge. Averaging both sides and fitting many settings beats one careful reading.",
"Students should know the magnetic force qv × B, circular motion and energy conservation. The demo follows the PASCO SE-{#} apparatus and its manual (N = {#}, R = {#} mm, up to {#} V, {#} A supply). Decide whether students should enter their own coil diameters or use the manual's R. Turn on <strong>Blind</strong> before projecting the table.",
"<strong>No field: a straight beam.</strong> This is where the manual starts: {#} V, no coil current. Raise the coil current slowly and watch the beam curl upward. Ask which way the force points and why the speed never changes.",
"<strong>A circle.</strong> Ask Q1 first. Show the view through the front coil: the beam and its reflection only line up when your eye is straight in front of the edge you read. Read the left side, move the eye, read the right side.",
"<strong>More voltage, bigger circle</strong> and <strong>More current, smaller circle.</strong> Predict the new radius each time (Q2) before switching.",
"<strong>Tilt the tube: a helix.</strong> Part of the velocity is along B and is untouched. Ask why a tilted tube spoils the measurement (Q5).",
"<strong>Measure e/m.</strong> Five simulated readings at the settings of the manual's sample data fill the table; have students add their own and compare the fit with the accepted value.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: the electrons in the beam move at constant speed even though a force acts on them all the time. Why? Which way does the force point at the top of the circle?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> At V = {#} V and I = {#} A, with N = {#} and a = {#} cm, find B, the electron speed and the radius of the circle. What radius do you expect at {#} V and the same current, and at {#} V and {#} A? Check with the demo.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> Why can this experiment give only e/m, and not e and m separately? What extra experiment did physicists need to get e itself?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> A student reads r = {#} cm when the true radius at {#} V and {#} A is {#} cm. What value of e/m do they get, and by what percentage is it off? Why is the error in e/m about twice the error in r?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> In <strong>Tilt the tube: a helix</strong> the tube is turned {#}°. What happens to the velocity component along B? Find the radius and pitch of the helix at {#} V and {#} A. If a student measured the helix as if it were a circle, would their e/m be too high or too low?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> The earth's field (take {#} × {#}⁻⁵ T along the coil axis) adds to or subtracts from the coil field. At {#} A, by what percentage does it change B, and e/m? How could you cancel it in the lab?",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> Real results for e/m often come out high. Give one physical reason the electrons could have less energy than eV when they reach the circle, and say which way that biases e/m.",
"<strong>\"The magnetic force speeds the electrons up.\"</strong> It is always at right angles to the velocity, so it does no work: it only turns the beam.",
"<strong>\"A bigger current makes the electrons go faster.\"</strong> The voltage sets the speed; the current sets the field and so the radius.",
"<strong>\"The beam is the electrons.\"</strong> The glow is helium atoms excited by collisions with the electrons; it marks the path the electrons take.",
"<strong>\"Reading one side carefully is enough.\"</strong> The beam may not be centered on the scale, and parallax shifts each side differently. Reading both sides and averaging removes much of that.",
"B = ({#}/{#})^({#}/{#}) μ₀NI/a ≈ {#} × {#}⁻⁴ T/A × I for N = {#}, a = {#} m. e/m = 2V/(B²r²); the fit is a least squares line of 2V against (Br)² with a free intercept: the slope is e/m and the intercept should come out near zero.",
"The beam is traced through the exact field of the two coils (computed with elliptic integrals), not a perfectly uniform one, so circles, helices and glass hits come from the computed motion. Off center the field is a little weaker, so e/m from the center field comes out slightly low for large circles (about {#}% for a {#} cm circle, about {#}% for a {#} cm circle).",
"The mirrored scale and the eye: the reading pointers are set by the student; the beam flickers by up to about {#} mm (simulated). Meter readings carry small seeded jitter.",
"Not simulated: energy lost by the electrons before they reach the circle (in the anode and in collisions with helium). The earth's field can be switched on or off.",
"Have students plot r against √V at fixed I and r against {#}/I at fixed V, and check both are straight lines before fitting for e/m.",
"History: J. J. Thomson measured e/m for cathode rays in {#} with crossed electric and magnetic fields and found it about a thousand times larger than for hydrogen ions, evidence that cathode rays are particles far lighter than atoms."
]);
